# Creating a client

The quick start reads everything from the environment with
`.environ_map = init.environ_map`. You can also set every option explicitly:

[]($code.siteAsset('snippets/client-init.zig').language('zig'))

Explicit options take precedence over environment variables, and environment
variables take precedence over the defaults. Empty or whitespace-only
environment values are ignored. `init` returns `error.MissingApiKey` if no key
is found.

| Variable | Default |
| --- | --- |
| `TYPESAFE_API_KEY` | none, required |
| `TYPESAFE_BASE_URL` | `https://api.typesafe.ai` |
| `TYPESAFE_DEFAULT_MODEL` | `jev-latest` |

The client wraps a `std.http.Client`, so connections are pooled and reused.
Requests can be issued from multiple threads.

# Retries

Failed requests are retried with exponential backoff and jitter. The defaults
match the official Python and JavaScript SDKs:

| Field | Default | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `max_retries` | `2` | Retries after the first attempt; `0` disables |
| `backoff_initial_ms` | `500` | First delay, doubled each retry |
| `backoff_max_ms` | `5000` | Cap on the backoff delay |
| `backoff_jitter` | `0.25` | Fraction of each delay randomly subtracted |
| `respect_retry_after` | `true` | Honor `Retry-After` / `retry-after-ms` |
| `max_retry_after_ms` | `60000` | Longer server delays fall back to backoff |
| `retry_connection_errors` | `true` | Retry when the server can't be reached |
| `retry_status` | 408, 429, 5xx | Function deciding which statuses to retry |

`429 Too Many Requests` and `529 Overloaded` are retried by default. Set the
policy on the client, as above, or per call with `.retry` (see
[per-call options]($link.page('dsl').ref('per-call-options'))). Use
`tai.RetryPolicy.none` to disable retries.

# Errors

Zig errors can't carry a payload, so `tai` returns a precise error and puts the
details in an optional `Diagnostics` value. This example is tested with an
invalid API key:

[]($code.siteAsset('snippets/errors.zig').language('zig'))

| Error | Cause |
| --- | --- |
| `BadRequest` | HTTP 400 |
| `Unauthorized` | HTTP 401: missing or invalid API key |
| `PermissionDenied` | HTTP 403 |
| `NotFound` | HTTP 404 |
| `RequestTimeout` | HTTP 408, after retries |
| `UnprocessableEntity` | HTTP 422: the body failed validation |
| `RateLimited` | HTTP 429, after retries |
| `Overloaded` | HTTP 529, after retries |
| `ServerError` | Other 5xx, after retries |
| `UnexpectedStatus` | Any other non-2xx status |
| `ConnectionFailed` | Couldn't connect, or the connection dropped |
| `InvalidBaseUrl` | `base_url` isn't an http(s) URL with a host |
| `InvalidResponse` | The body didn't match the documented shape, or an answer didn't match its question |
| `NoQuestions`, `DuplicateQuestionName`, ... | Client-side validation for runtime-built requests |

`Diagnostics` records:

- `status`: the last HTTP status, if a response arrived.
- `body`: the last non-2xx body, usually JSON such as `{"detail": {...}}`.
- `attempts`: how many attempts were made, including the first.
- `transport_error`: the underlying error behind `ConnectionFailed`.

Call `deinit` on it when you're done, since it owns a copy of the body.

# Listing models

`listModels` calls `GET /v1/models`, which lists the aliases your account can
use. Versioned ids such as `jev-1.13.0` are accepted in `model` whether or not
they're listed.

[]($code.siteAsset('snippets/list-models.zig').language('zig'))

# Timeouts

`std.http.Client` doesn't support per-request timeouts yet. Because every call
takes a `std.Io`, you can cancel it from outside. For example, run the call with
`io.concurrent` and call `cancel` on the future after a deadline; the call then
returns `error.Canceled`.
